dict(pas="19.1", w="子張=zǐ zhāng/Zizhang | 曰：「/said | ^士/an officer | 見=jiàn/seeing | 危/danger | 致/hands over | 命，/his life | 見=jiàn/seeing | 得/gain | 思/thinks of | 義，/what is right | ^祭=jì/at the sacrifice | 思/(he) thinks of | 敬，/reverence | 喪=sāng/in mourning | 思/(he) thinks of | 哀，/grief | ^其/surely | 可/(he) will do | 已/(and that is all) | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="Zizhang said:\n“An officer, seeing danger, hands over his life; seeing gain, he thinks of what is right;\nat the sacrifice he thinks of reverence; in mourning he thinks of grief —\nhe will surely do, and that is all.”",
  i="Zizhang said: “An officer who will lay down his life in the face of danger, who thinks of what is right when there is gain in sight, who thinks of reverence when he sacrifices and of grief when he mourns — such a man will pass.”",
  n="The book is named 子張 from its opening words. Zhu Xi's heading: the whole book records the sayings of disciples, Zixia's the most and Zigong's next (此篇皆記弟子之言而子夏為多子貢次之); for after Yan Hui none in the school was quicker to understand than Zigong, and after Master Zeng none more solid than Zixia (盖孔門自顔子以下頴悟莫若子貢自曽子以下篤實無若子夏), so their words are recorded in detail. He counts twenty-five chapters (凡二十五章); our numbers are his. Zizhang is the disciple Zhuansun Shi 顓孫師 (2.18). 士 'an officer', a man of service (4.9, 13.20). 見 jiàn 'to see', twice. 致命 'to hand over one's life' — Zhu Xi: to give up one's life entirely, like saying 授命 (致命謂委致其命猶言授命也), the word of 14.13 見危授命 'seeing danger, he gives up his life'; Kong Anguo in He Yan: not to grudge one's own person (致命不愛其身也). 見得思義 'seeing gain, he thinks of what is right' is the last of the gentleman's nine thoughts at 16.10. 祭 jì 'to sacrifice'; 喪 sāng 'mourning'. Each clause is a situation followed by what the officer does in it, with the subject 士 left to carry over. 其可已矣: 其 'surely' softens the verdict, 可 'acceptable, will do', and 已矣 'and that is all'. Zhu Xi: these four are the great points on which a man's standing rests (四者立身之大節); fall short in one and nothing else about him is worth looking at (一有不至則餘無足觀), so Zizhang says an officer who can do these is nearly there (庶乎其可矣)."),
# ---------------- 19.2 ----------------
dict(pas="19.2", w="子張=zǐ zhāng/Zizhang | 曰：「/said | ^執/holding to | 德/virtue | 不/(but) not | 𢎞，=hóng/broadly | 信/trusting | 道=dào/the Way | 不/(but) not | 篤，/firmly | ^焉=yān/how | 能/can | 為=wéi/(he) count as | 有？/having (it) | 焉=yān/how | 能/can | 為=wéi/(he) count as | 亡？」=wú/lacking (it)",
  l="Zizhang said:\n“Holding to virtue, but not broadly; trusting the Way, but not firmly —\nhow can he count as having it? how can he count as lacking it?”",
  i="Zizhang said: “A man who keeps hold of virtue but in a narrow grip, and trusts the Way but without firmness — can you say he has them? Can you say he has not? He weighs nothing either way.”",
  n="𢎞 is the edition's form of 弘 hóng 'broad' (8.7). 執 'to hold on to'; 篤 'firm, solid'. Each of the first two clauses sets a verb against a 不 that takes back half of it: he holds virtue, not broadly; he trusts the Way, not firmly. 焉 is yān 'how' (Zhu Xi: 焉於虔反), and 亡 is read as 無 wú 'not to have' (亡讀作無下同, 'and so below', at 19.5). 為 wéi 'to count as'. Zhu Xi: if a man keeps what he has gained too narrowly, his virtue stands alone (有所得而守之太狹則德孤); if he trusts what he has heard without firmness, the Way falls away (有所聞而信之不篤則道廢). 焉能為有，焉能為亡 is, he says, like saying 'not enough to make anything lighter or heavier' (猶言不足為輕重): whether such a man has them or not makes no difference. Kong Anguo in He Yan reads it the same way (言無所輕重也)."),
