dict(pas="19.4", w="子夏=zǐ xià/Zixia | 曰：「/said | ^雖/even | 小/small | 道，=dào/ways | 必/surely | 有/(there) is | 可/worth | 觀/seeing | 者/something | 焉；=yān/in them | ^致/carried | 逺=yuǎn/far | 恐/(one) fears | 泥，=nì/getting stuck | 是/this | 以/is why | 君子=jūn zǐ/the gentleman | 不/does not | 為=wéi/practise (them) | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="Zixia said:\n“Even in small ways there is surely something worth seeing;\nbut carried far, one fears getting stuck — this is why the gentleman does not practise them.”",
  i="Zixia said: “Even the lesser arts are sure to have something worth looking at. But follow them a long way and you are likely to get bogged down, and that is why the gentleman does not take them up.”",
  n="雖 'even, although'. 小道 'small ways' — Zhu Xi: things like farming and gardening, medicine and divination (小道如農圃醫卜之屬); He Yan's unsigned comment: other doctrines (小道謂異端也, the 異端 of 2.16). 可觀者 'something worth seeing', and 焉 yān 'in them' (the fused 於之). 致逺 'carried far'. 泥 is nì 'to get stuck' (Zhu Xi: 泥去聲…泥不通也, 'not getting through'; Bao Xian in He Yan: 泥難不通也). 是以 'this is why'. 為 wéi 'to practise, to take up'. Zhu Xi quotes Yang Shi: the hundred schools and the many skills are like ears, eyes, mouth and nose — each is clear in its own field but they cannot reach one another (百家衆技猶耳目口鼻皆有所明而不能相通); not that they have nothing worth seeing, but carried far they get stuck, and so the gentleman does not practise them."),
