dict(pas="19.5", w="子夏=zǐ xià/Zixia | 曰：「/said | ^日/each day | 知=zhī/knowing | 其/what he | 所/(that which) | 亡，=wú/lacks | 月/each month | 無/not | 忘/forgetting | 其/what he | 所/(that which) | 能，/can do | ^可/(he) can | 謂/be called | 好=hào/fond of | 學/learning | 也/(assertion) | 已/(and that is all) | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="Zixia said:\n“Each day knowing what he lacks, each month not forgetting what he can do —\nhe can be called fond of learning, and that is all.”",
  i="Zixia said: “A man who each day comes to know something he lacked, and month after month never forgets what he has mastered, may truly be called fond of learning.”",
  n="日 'each day' and 月 'each month' stand before their verbs as adverbs. 所 makes a noun of what follows: 其所亡 'what he lacks', 其所能 'what he can do'. 亡 is wú 'not to have' (Zhu Xi: 亡讀作無; 亡無也謂己之所未有, 'what one does not yet have'); Kong Anguo in He Yan takes it as what one has not yet heard of (日知其所未聞也). 好 hào 'fond of' (好去聲). 可謂好學也已矣 closes as 1.14 does (可謂好學也已), with a further 矣. Zhu Xi quotes Yin Tun: one who is fond of learning renews himself daily and loses nothing (好學者日新而不失)."),
# ---------------- 19.6 ----------------
dict(pas="19.6", w="子夏=zǐ xià/Zixia | 曰：「/said | ^博/broadly | 學/learning | 而/and | 篤/firmly | 志，/setting one's aim | 切=qiè/earnestly | 問/asking | 而/and | 近/near at hand | 思，/thinking | ^仁/humaneness | 在/is | 其/it | 中/within | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="Zixia said:\n“Learning broadly and setting one's aim firmly, asking earnestly and thinking of what is near at hand —\nhumaneness is there within it.”",
  i="Zixia said: “Learn widely and hold your aim steady; ask about what presses on you, and think about what lies close at hand: humaneness is to be found right there.”",
  n="Four verbs, each with an adverb before it, in two pairs joined by 而: 博學 'learning broadly' (6.25, 12.15), 篤志 'making one's aim firm', 切問 'asking earnestly' (切 qiè, as 切切 13.28), 近思 'thinking near'. 仁在其中矣 'humaneness is there within it', the shape of 2.18 and 15.31 (禄在其中矣). Zhu Xi: all four are matters of learning, asking, thinking and sifting, not yet the vigorous practice that makes humaneness (四者皆學問思辨之事耳未及乎力行而為仁也); but whoever works at them keeps his heart from racing outward, and what he holds ripens of itself — so 'humaneness is within it' (然從事於此則心不外馳而所存自熟故曰仁在其中矣). He quotes Master Cheng: thinking near means reasoning onward by kind (近思者以類而推); and a Mr Su: learning broadly with an aim not firm, one is large and achieves nothing; asking widely and thinking far, one toils without result (博學而志不篤則大而無成泛問逺思則勞而無功). Kong Anguo in He Yan glosses the first pair 博學而厚識也 'to learn broadly and retain much', apparently taking 志 as 'to keep in mind'. An unsigned comment there: earnest asking is asking about what one has learned and not yet grasped, and near thinking is thinking about what one is able to reach."),
